\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{The titles of the publications are to be set in Figtree; however, this may not be possible, as Figtree lacks Greek characters, math symbols, \etc  ., that may appear in the titles. Therefore, you may need to change the font you are using for such a title. Papers E and F show two approaches for doing this. Paper E uses math mode to get the Greek characters, while Paper F uses a font that has the Greek characters. Further note that \textbackslash mystixmathfont has been used, rather than a font such as \textbackslash NotoSansFont - as the overbar in the latter font is too low. The font could have been applied to the whole title, but this would result in serifed letters for the whole title. Both of these commands for fonts were introduced in \texttt  {kththesis.cls}.}{xxv}{section*.38}\protected@file@percent }
\savepicturepage{pgfid33}{25}
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid33}{21520668}{22217355}
\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{The use of math mode in the title of Paper E will generate a warning "Font shape `U/stmry/m/n' in size <5.5> not available" - since scaling the 11pt math font for the superscripts and subscripts leads to 5.5pt letters - for which LaTeX substitutes 5pt letters. Both for this font scaling issue and to improve readability (see, for example, the fictitious title of Report A), I would think that the publication titles should be set in a font larger than 11pt.}{xxv}{section*.39}\protected@file@percent }
\savepicturepage{pgfid34}{25}
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid34}{21520668}{13939158}
